1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:16,091
[Host] Welcome to Japanese Daily News. I'm Maya. Today is June twenty-first. For high-school students looking for work, this is the best job market on record — there are more openings than ever. Let's listen.

2
00:00:16,692 --> 00:00:19,452
[News] 今日のニュースです。

3
00:00:20,152 --> 00:00:27,832
[News] 来年の春に高校を卒業する人の就職が、とても良い状況です。

4
00:00:28,532 --> 00:00:36,787
[News] 会社が「働いてほしい」と言う数が、高校生一人に対してたくさんあります。

5
00:00:37,487 --> 00:00:44,496
[News] 一人の高校生に、平均で4つ以上の仕事があるそうです。

6
00:00:45,195 --> 00:00:53,619
[News] これは、調査を始めた1988年から、一番多い数です。

7
00:00:54,320 --> 00:01:00,344
[News] 理由は、多くの会社で、人手が足りないからです。

8
00:01:01,043 --> 00:01:07,115
[News] 日本では、若い人の数が少しずつ減っています。

9
00:01:07,816 --> 00:01:14,512
[News] だから、会社は高校生にも、来てほしいと思っています。

10
00:01:15,212 --> 00:01:22,436
[News] 給料を、大学を出た人と同じくらいに上げる会社も出てきました。

11
00:01:23,135 --> 00:01:30,096
[News] 高校生にとっては、仕事を選びやすい、うれしいニュースです。

12
00:01:30,796 --> 00:01:32,427
[Host] Let's break it down.

13
00:01:33,028 --> 00:01:41,811
[Slow] 来年の春に高校を卒業する人の就職が、とても良い状況です。

14
00:01:42,611 --> 00:01:47,243
[Host] Job-hunting looks very good for people graduating high school next spring.

15
00:01:47,843 --> 00:01:50,316
[Vocab] 就職

16
00:01:50,715 --> 00:01:56,355
[Host] It means finding a job, or employment — landing your first position after finishing school.

17
00:01:56,956 --> 00:02:06,388
[Slow] 会社が「働いてほしい」と言う数が、高校生一人に対してたくさんあります。

18
00:02:07,188 --> 00:02:12,756
[Host] There are many companies saying 'we want you to work for us' for each single high-school student.

19
00:02:13,355 --> 00:02:15,804
[Vocab] 働く

20
00:02:16,204 --> 00:02:21,580
[Host] It means to work. A related noun means labor — the work itself.

21
00:02:22,180 --> 00:02:30,171
[Slow] 一人の高校生に、平均で4つ以上の仕事があるそうです。

22
00:02:30,972 --> 00:02:36,372
[Host] I hear there are, on average, more than four jobs for each high-school student.

23
00:02:36,972 --> 00:02:39,276
[Vocab] 仕事

24
00:02:39,675 --> 00:02:45,123
[Host] It means a job, or work — what you do all day, and what you look for when job-hunting.

25
00:02:45,723 --> 00:02:55,348
[Slow] これは、調査を始めた1988年から、一番多い数です。

26
00:02:56,147 --> 00:03:00,563
[Host] This is the highest number since the survey began in nineteen eighty-eight.

27
00:03:01,163 --> 00:03:03,204
[Vocab] 数

28
00:03:03,604 --> 00:03:09,027
[Host] It means number or count — here, the number of jobs available for each student.

29
00:03:09,627 --> 00:03:16,491
[Slow] 理由は、多くの会社で、人手が足りないからです。

30
00:03:17,292 --> 00:03:21,036
[Host] The reason is that many companies don't have enough workers.

31
00:03:21,635 --> 00:03:23,795
[Vocab] 人手

32
00:03:24,195 --> 00:03:31,443
[Host] It means manpower, or helping hands. Being short of hands is a phrase you'll hear all over Japan now.

33
00:03:32,044 --> 00:03:38,979
[Slow] 日本では、若い人の数が少しずつ減っています。

34
00:03:39,780 --> 00:03:44,436
[Host] In Japan, the number of young people is gradually decreasing.

35
00:03:45,036 --> 00:03:47,003
[Vocab] 減る

36
00:03:47,403 --> 00:03:53,211
[Host] It means to decrease, to go down in number. It's the opposite of the verb meaning to increase.

37
00:03:53,812 --> 00:04:01,467
[Slow] だから、会社は高校生にも、来てほしいと思っています。

38
00:04:02,268 --> 00:04:07,259
[Host] So companies want high-school students, too, to come and join them.

39
00:04:07,860 --> 00:04:09,996
[Vocab] 思う

40
00:04:10,395 --> 00:04:16,684
[Host] It means to think, or to feel — the verb you reach for to give an opinion, as in 'I think that ....'

41
00:04:17,283 --> 00:04:25,540
[Slow] 給料を、大学を出た人と同じくらいに上げる会社も出てきました。

42
00:04:26,339 --> 00:04:32,339
[Host] Some companies have even started raising pay to about the same as university graduates get.

43
00:04:32,939 --> 00:04:35,244
[Vocab] 給料

44
00:04:35,644 --> 00:04:42,220
[Host] It means salary, or pay. The day it lands is payday, and when it climbs, you've gotten a raise.

45
00:04:42,819 --> 00:04:45,819
[Host] Let's listen to the full story one more time.

46
00:04:46,420 --> 00:04:49,180
[News] 今日のニュースです。

47
00:04:49,879 --> 00:04:57,560
[News] 来年の春に高校を卒業する人の就職が、とても良い状況です。

48
00:04:58,259 --> 00:05:06,516
[News] 会社が「働いてほしい」と言う数が、高校生一人に対してたくさんあります。

49
00:05:07,216 --> 00:05:14,223
[News] 一人の高校生に、平均で4つ以上の仕事があるそうです。

50
00:05:14,923 --> 00:05:23,348
[News] これは、調査を始めた1988年から、一番多い数です。

51
00:05:24,048 --> 00:05:30,072
[News] 理由は、多くの会社で、人手が足りないからです。

52
00:05:30,771 --> 00:05:36,843
[News] 日本では、若い人の数が少しずつ減っています。

53
00:05:37,543 --> 00:05:44,240
[News] だから、会社は高校生にも、来てほしいと思っています。

54
00:05:44,939 --> 00:05:52,163
[News] 給料を、大学を出た人と同じくらいに上げる会社も出てきました。

55
00:05:52,863 --> 00:05:59,824
[News] 高校生にとっては、仕事を選びやすい、うれしいニュースです。

56
00:06:00,524 --> 00:06:03,259
[Host] Quiz time. Three questions.

57
00:06:03,860 --> 00:06:12,403
[Slow] 来年の春に高校を卒業する人の就職は、どんな状況ですか。

58
00:06:13,204 --> 00:06:20,668
[Host] What's the job situation for people graduating high school next spring? Very good — lots of openings for each student.

59
00:06:21,267 --> 00:06:28,228
[Slow] 高校生一人に、平均でいくつの仕事がありますか。

60
00:06:29,028 --> 00:06:34,500
[Host] On average, how many jobs are there per high-school student? More than four.

61
00:06:35,100 --> 00:06:40,596
[Slow] 仕事がたくさんある理由は、何ですか。

62
00:06:41,396 --> 00:06:47,923
[Host] Why are there so many jobs? Because companies are short of workers, and young people are decreasing.

63
00:06:48,524 --> 00:07:00,908
[Host] That's today's Japanese Daily News. Full transcript with furigana at podcast.jpnotes.dev. Subscribe on Apple Podcasts or Spotify. See you tomorrow.

64
00:07:01,507 --> 00:07:03,764
[News] また明日。
